==================================================================
DAY 20 - PART B: LAB
Inspect a CNI, then build a cluster with Calico
Time: 90 minutes
==================================================================

STEP 1: Inspect kindnet on a node
---------------------------------
k get ds -n kube-system
k get pods -n kube-system -l app=kindnet -o wide

docker exec lab-worker ls /etc/cni/net.d/
docker exec lab-worker cat /etc/cni/net.d/10-kindnet.conflist
docker exec lab-worker ls /opt/cni/bin/

In the conflist find: "type" (ptp / host-local), and the "ranges"
(this node's PodCIDR).


STEP 2: See the CNI being called
--------------------------------
k run cnitest --image=nginx:1.27
k describe pod cnitest | grep -i -E "sandbox|network"
docker exec $(k get pod cnitest -o jsonpath='{.spec.nodeName}') \
  journalctl -u containerd --no-pager | grep -i cni | tail -5
k delete pod cnitest


STEP 3: Create a second cluster WITHOUT a CNI
---------------------------------------------
cd ~/k8s-journey/kind
vi kind-calico.yaml

kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
networking:
  disableDefaultCNI: true
  podSubnet: 192.168.0.0/16
nodes:
- role: control-plane
- role: worker
- role: worker

kind create cluster --name calico --config kind-calico.yaml
k get nodes                 # NotReady!
k describe node calico-worker | grep -A3 -i "ready"
k get pods -n kube-system   # coredns Pending

Without a CNI, nodes are NotReady and pods can't get IPs.
(Your kubectl context switched to kind-calico. Check: kubectx)


STEP 4: Install Calico
----------------------
Find the current Calico version at
https://docs.tigera.io/calico/latest/getting-started/kubernetes/quickstart
and set it (example below; use the latest shown there):

CALICO=v3.30.3
k create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/$CALICO/manifests/operator-crds.yaml
k create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/$CALICO/manifests/tigera-operator.yaml
k create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/$CALICO/manifests/custom-resources.yaml

(If operator-crds.yaml returns 404 for your version, skip that line;
older versions ship the CRDs inside tigera-operator.yaml.)

k get pods -n calico-system -w      # wait until all Running (2-5 min)
k get nodes                         # Ready


STEP 5: Compare with kindnet
----------------------------
k create deploy net --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=4
k get pods -o wide                  # IPs from 192.168.x.x

docker exec calico-worker ip route | head
docker exec calico-worker ls /etc/cni/net.d/
docker exec calico-worker ip -d link show | grep -E "vxlan|tunl"

Note the cali* interfaces and routes to other nodes' blocks
(via vxlan.calico or tunl0 depending on the mode).


STEP 6: Calico's own objects
----------------------------
k get ippools.crd.projectcalico.org -o yaml | grep -E "cidr|encapsulation|natOutgoing"
k get installation default -o yaml | grep -A8 calicoNetwork

Keep this cluster: Week 5 Day 6 (NetworkPolicy) uses a CNI that
enforces policies.


STEP 7 (OPTIONAL): Cilium
-------------------------
Cilium has a CLI that installs it:
  CILIUM_CLI_VERSION=$(curl -s https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cilium/cilium-cli/main/stable.txt)
  curl -L --remote-name-all https://github.com/cilium/cilium-cli/releases/download/${CILIUM_CLI_VERSION}/cilium-linux-amd64.tar.gz
  sudo tar xzvf cilium-linux-amd64.tar.gz -C /usr/local/bin
Create a third kind cluster with disableDefaultCNI: true, then:
  cilium install
  cilium status --wait
  cilium connectivity test     (takes a while; very educational)
Delete it afterwards to save RAM: kind delete cluster --name <name>


STEP 8: Switch back and clean up
--------------------------------
k delete deploy net
kubectx kind-lab
k get nodes
To free memory you can delete the calico cluster and recreate it in
Week 5:
  kind delete cluster --name calico
